      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\), where \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\) are the midpoints of edges \\(AC\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(DC\\) respectively in a right prism \\(ABCDEF\\) with an equilateral triangle base \\(ABC\\).\n\nFirst, let's identify the coordinates of the vertices of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\). We can place the triangle in the coordinate system such that:\n- \\(A = (0, 0)\\),\n- \\(B = (12, 0)\\),\n- \\(C = (6, -6)\\).\n\nSince \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\) are the midpoints of \\(AC\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(DC\\) respectively, we can find their coordinates as follows:\n- \\(X\\) is the midpoint of \\(AC\\), so \\(X = \\left( \\frac{0+6}{2}, \\frac{0-6}{2} \\right) = (3, -3)\\).\n- \\(Y\\) is the midpoint of \\(BC\\), so \\(Y = \\left( \\frac{12+6}{2}, \\frac{0-6}{2} \\right) = (9, -3)\\).\n- \\(Z\\) is the midpoint of \\(DC\\), so \\(Z = \\left( \\frac{6+0}{2}, \\frac{-6-6}{2} \\right) = (3, -6)\\).\n\nNow, we need to calculate the lengths of the sides of triangle \\(XYZ\\) using the distance formula \\(d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\n1. The distance \\(XY\\):\n\\[\nXY = \\sqrt{(9-3)^2 + (-3+3)^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\n\\]\n\n2. The distance \\(YZ\\):\n\\[\nYZ = \\sqrt{(3-9)^2 + (-6+3)^2} = \\sqrt{(-6)^2 + (-3)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 9} = \\sqrt{45} = 3\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\n\n3. The distance \\(ZX\\):\n\\[\nZX = \\sqrt{(3-3)^2 + (-6+3)^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3)^2} = \\sqrt{9} = 3.\n\\]\n\nThe perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is the sum of the lengths of its sides:\n\\[\nXY + YZ + ZX = 6 + 3\\sqrt{5} + 3 = 9 + 3\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is \\(\\boxed{9 + 3\\sqrt{5}}\\).",
