      "text": "To find the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\), we need to determine the lengths of its sides. Points \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\) are the midpoints of edges \\(AC\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(DC\\) respectively. Therefore, we can use the properties of midpoints and equilateral triangles to find the necessary lengths.\n\nFirst, let's identify the coordinates of the vertices of the equilateral triangle \\(ABC\\). Assume the base \\(BC\\) lies along the x-axis with \\(B = (12, 0)\\) and \\(C = (6, -6)\\). Then, the height of the equilateral triangle can be calculated using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{12^2 - 6^2} = \\sqrt{144 - 36} = \\sqrt{108} = 6\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the coordinates of point \\(A\\) are \\((0, 6\\sqrt{3})\\). Now, we can find the coordinates of points \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\).\n\n- \\(X\\) is the midpoint of \\(AC\\):\n\\[\nX = \\left( \\frac{0+6}{2}, \\frac{6\\sqrt{3} - 6}{2} \\right) = (3, 3\\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\n- \\(Y\\) is the midpoint of \\(BC\\):\n\\[\nY = \\left( \\frac{12+6}{2}, \\frac{0 - 6}{2} \\right) = (9, -3)\n\\]\n\n- \\(Z\\) is the midpoint of \\(DC\\):\n\\[\nZ = \\left( \\frac{12+6}{2}, \\frac{0 - 6}{2} \\right) = (9, -3)\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the lengths of the sides of triangle \\(XYZ\\). We use the distance formula \\(d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\\).\n\n- The distance \\(XY\\):\n\\[\nXY = \\sqrt{(9 - 3)^2 + (-3 - 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + (-3 - 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + (3 + 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 9 + 18\\sqrt{3} + 27} = \\sqrt{72 + 18\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\n- The distance \\(YZ\\):\n\\[\nYZ = \\sqrt{(9 - 9)^2 + (-3 + 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{0 + (-3 + 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{9 - 18\\sqrt{3} + 27} = \\sqrt{36 - 18\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\n- The distance \\(ZX\\):\n\\[\nZX = \\sqrt{(9 - 3)^2 + (-3 - 3\\sqrt{3} + 3\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is:\n\\[\nXY + YZ + ZX = \\sqrt{72 + 18\\sqrt{3}} + \\sqrt{36 - 18\\sqrt{